/**

\page preamble Preamble

Preamble
========

Peano is an open source framework for solvers on dynamically adaptive
Cartesian meshes. Its core is built with C++, but many tools around it
are written in Python.  is based upon the fact that spacetrees, a
generalisation of the classical octree concept, yield a cascade of
adaptive Cartesian grids. Consequently, any spacetree traversal is
equivalent to an element-wise traversal of the hierarchy of the adaptive
Cartesian grids. The software  realises such a grid traversal and
storage algorithm, and it provides hook-in points for applications
performing per-element, per-vertex, and so forth operations on the grid.
It also provides interfaces for dynamic load balancing, sophisticated
geometry representations, and other features.

Peano is currently available in its fourth generation. The development
of the original set of Peano codes started around 2002. 2005-2009, we
merged these codes into one Peano kernel (2nd generation). In 2009, I
started a complete reimplementation of the kernel with special emphasis
on reusability, application-independent design and the support for rapid
prototyping. This third generation of the code ended around 2019 when we
released the ExaHyPE code---a hyperbolic equation system solver engine
which uses Peano's AMR meshes. The current guidebook discusses  , which
reuses ideas and lessons learned from Peano 3 as well as lots of code
buildling blocks, but can be seen as a major rewrite starting from
scratch.

 has been the code base for multiple projects over the past years. The
most prominent one is likely ExaHyPE. The first generation of ExaHyPE
has been a stand-alone projects using Peano as one required library or
framework out of many. The second generation, , is merged into as
spin-off. This document therefore covers , too.

Dependencies and prerequisites {#dependencies-and-prerequisites .unnumbered}
------------------------------

's core is plain C++17 code. We however use a whole set of tools around
it:

-   GNU autotools (automake) to set up the system (required).

-   C++17-compatible C++ compiler (required).

-   Python3 (optional; not required if you work only with the C++
    baseline).

-   MPI2 (optional). MPI's multithreaded support is required.

-   Intel's Threading Building Blocks (TBB) or OpenMP 5. We also can
    work with C++ threads, but found their performance suboptimal. TBB
    is not shipped as part of OneAPI.

-   The Visualization Toolkit (VTK) if you want to use the built-in
    visualisation facilities (optional).

-   Doxygen if you want to create HTML pages of PDFs of the in-code
    documentation.

I test and maintain  for Linux only. If you prefer Windows or MacOS, it
should work as long as you provide the mandatory tools from above, but I
won't be able to help.

  Software     Version        Remarks
  ------------ -------------- -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  GNU g++      7.5 or older   Works with  core but not with some extensions such as .
  GNU g++      9.3.1          Current development version. Works.
  Intel icpc   19.1           Works with  and all extensions such as . Tests with Intel Fortran have been successful, too.
  VTK          7--9           Supported by . Default is 8, i.e. if you need other version, you have to inform configure (see Chapter [\[chapter:postprocessing\]](#chapter:postprocessing){reference-type="ref" reference="chapter:postprocessing"}).
  Python       3              Development version. Python 2 is not supported by frontend.

How the text is organised {#how-the-text-is-organised .unnumbered}
-------------------------

This LaTeX documentation first summarises some installation options. We
then present some high-level tutorials for some applications built on
top of . This notably covers . We continue with a discussion of 's
Python API, before I discuss C++ options and how to extend the
integration of Python with the C++ framework. An appendix gives further
information on particular machines, file formats, and so forth. It hosts
an FAQ and tips how to combine/use  with other tools.

While the tutorial present a user's view, i.e. how to use
pre-manufactured ingredients, the latter chapters discuss how to
implement new ingredients. However, they still give a high-level
overview and focus on rationale and design decisions. 's core philosophy
is *documentation is in the code* (see Section
[\[section:installation:documentation\]](#section:installation:documentation){reference-type="ref"
reference="section:installation:documentation"}).

In line with this philosophy, also the LaTeX documentation is held
within the repository. If you feel something is urgently missing and
deserves a chapter or things remain unclear, please extend the
LaTeX documentation and issue a merge request.

\
Tobias Weinzierl\

*/